## Keep NGINX and OS Updated

Always update: Regularly update NGINX to the latest stable version. New releases often include security patches for discovered vulnerabilities.
Operating System: Ensure your underlying operating system is also kept up-to-date with the latest security patches.

## Principle of Least Privilege

Run as unprivileged user: Configure NGINX to run as a non-privileged user (e.g., www-data or nginx) instead of root.

```nginx
# In /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
user nginx; # Or www-data, etc.
```


File Permissions: Set strict file permissions for NGINX configuration files, logs, and web roots. Only the NGINX user should have read access to configuration files, and only the NGINX user (or processes it needs to interact with) should have write access to logs.
Minimize Information Disclosure:
Disable server_tokens: Prevent NGINX from revealing its version number and operating system in HTTP headers and error pages.
Nginx
# In /etc/nginx/nginx.conf, inside http, server, or location block
server_tokens off;


Custom Error Pages: Configure custom error pages (e.g., 404, 500) to avoid disclosing server details through default error messages.
Secure SSL/TLS Configuration:
Force HTTPS with HSTS: Redirect all HTTP traffic to HTTPS and enable HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) to force browsers to always connect via HTTPS for a specified duration.
Nginx
# Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name your_domain.com www.your_domain.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

# HTTPS server block
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name your_domain.com www.your_domain.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/your_domain.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/ssl/your_domain.key;

    # HSTS (max-age for 1 year, include subdomains, preload for browser preloading)
    add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;

    # Strong TLS protocols (TLSv1.2 and TLSv1.3 are current best practice)
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    # Secure cipher suites (prioritize PFS)
    ssl_ciphers 'TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384:TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-SHA256';
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;

    # OCSP Stapling (improves performance and security)
    ssl_stapling on;
    ssl_stapling_verify on;
    ssl_trusted_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/your_ca_bundle.crt; # Your CA bundle

    # SSL session caching for performance
    ssl_session_cache shared:SSL:10m;
    ssl_session_timeout 1d;

    # Generate strong Diffie-Hellman parameters
    # openssl dhparam -out /etc/nginx/ssl/dhparam.pem 4096
    ssl_dhparam /etc/nginx/ssl/dhparam.pem;
}


Obtain SSL Certificates: Use trusted Certificate Authorities (CAs) like Let's Encrypt for free, automated certificates.
Disable Unnecessary Modules and Features:
When compiling NGINX from source, disable modules you don't need to reduce the attack surface.
Remove or comment out unused directives in your configuration (e.g., autoindex, charset, userdir if not needed).
Control Resources and Limits (DoS Protection):
Buffer Sizes: Limit buffer sizes to prevent potential Denial of Service (DoS) attacks.
Nginx
client_body_buffer_size 16k;
client_header_buffer_size 1k;
client_max_body_size 8m; # Adjust based on your application's needs
large_client_header_buffers 4 8k;


Timeout Settings: Configure reasonable timeouts to prevent slowloris attacks and keep-alive connections from consuming resources.
Nginx
client_body_timeout 10s;
client_header_timeout 10s;
keepalive_timeout 5s;
send_timeout 10s;


Limit Connections/Requests: Implement rate limiting to protect against brute-force attacks and mitigate DDoS.
Nginx
# Define a zone for rate limiting by IP address
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=mylimit:10m rate=5r/s;

server {
    # ...
    location /login/ {
        limit_req zone=mylimit burst=10 nodelay; # Allow burst of 10 requests, no delay for initial burst
        # ...
    }
}


Disable Unnecessary HTTP Methods:
If your application only uses GET and POST, disable other HTTP methods like PUT, DELETE, CONNECT, etc.
Nginx
if ($request_method !~ ^(GET|HEAD|POST)$) {
    return 405; # Method Not Allowed
}


Implement Security Headers:
X-Frame-Options: Prevents clickjacking by controlling whether your site can be embedded in iframes.
Nginx
add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"; # or DENY


X-Content-Type-Options: Prevents MIME-sniffing attacks.
Nginx
add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff";


X-XSS-Protection: Enables the browser's XSS filter.
Nginx
add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block";


Content Security Policy (CSP): A powerful header that helps prevent XSS and data injection attacks by specifying allowed sources for content. This can be complex to implement correctly but offers significant protection.
Nginx
add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' www.google-analytics.com; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self';";


Referrer-Policy: Controls how much referrer information is sent with requests.
Nginx
add_header Referrer-Policy "no-referrer-when-downgrade";


Secure Sensitive Directories and Files:
Block access to hidden files: Prevent access to .git, .svn, .ht* files.
Nginx
location ~ /\.ht {
    deny all;
}
location ~ /\.git {
    deny all;
}
location ~ /\.svn {
    deny all;
}


Restrict access to admin interfaces: Use allow/deny directives or HTTP Basic Authentication to protect sensitive areas like /admin or /wp-admin.
Nginx
location /wp-admin/ {
    allow 192.168.1.0/24; # Allow from specific IP range
    allow 203.0.113.1; # Allow from specific IP
    deny all;
}


Disable directory listing: Ensure autoindex off; is set in your nginx.conf to prevent directory Browse.
Logging and Monitoring:
Configure comprehensive logging: Ensure NGINX access and error logs are enabled and properly configured.
Monitor logs: Regularly review logs for suspicious activity, errors, and potential attacks. Consider using a centralized logging solution (e.g., ELK stack, Splunk) or tools like Fail2Ban.
II. System-Level Security
Firewall (e.g., ufw, firewalld, iptables):
Only allow necessary ports (e.g., 80, 443, 22 for SSH) and restrict access to specific IP addresses where possible.
Block known malicious IPs or ranges.
Fail2Ban:
Install and configure Fail2Ban to monitor NGINX logs for suspicious patterns (e.g., repeated failed login attempts, unusual request rates) and automatically ban the offending IP addresses using firewall rules.
SELinux/AppArmor:
Utilize mandatory access control (MAC) systems like SELinux (for RHEL/CentOS) or AppArmor (for Debian/Ubuntu) to confine NGINX processes and restrict their capabilities, even if compromised. This adds another layer of defense.
File Integrity Monitoring (FIM):
Implement FIM tools (e.g., AIDE, OSSEC) to detect unauthorized changes to NGINX configuration files, web content, and system binaries.
Rootkit Detection:
Regularly scan your system for rootkits using tools like chkrootkit and rkhunter.
Secure SSH Access:
Use strong, unique SSH passwords or, even better, SSH keys.
Disable password authentication for SSH.
Disable root login over SSH.
Change the default SSH port.
Containerization (Docker/Kubernetes):
If using containers, ensure your NGINX containers are built with security in mind (minimal base images, non-root users, restricted capabilities). Containerization provides isolation but doesn't replace the need for strong NGINX configuration.
III. Web Application Firewall (WAF)
ModSecurity: Integrate a WAF like ModSecurity with NGINX. ModSecurity can provide an additional layer of protection by analyzing HTTP traffic for malicious patterns (e.g., SQL injection, XSS) and blocking attacks before they reach your backend application.
Install ModSecurity and its NGINX connector.
Load OWASP ModSecurity Core Rule Set (CRS) or other relevant rule sets.
Tune the rules to avoid false positives for your specific applications.
IV. Regular Auditing and Testing
Security Audits: Regularly perform security audits of your NGINX configuration and underlying system.
Penetration Testing: Conduct periodic penetration tests to identify potential vulnerabilities.
Vulnerability Scanners: Use automated vulnerability scanners (e.g., Nessus, OpenVAS, Nikto) to scan your public-facing NGINX instances.
SSL/TLS Checkers: Use online tools like Qualys SSL Labs to check your SSL/TLS configuration and ensure you achieve a high rating (A+).
Test Configuration: Always test your NGINX configuration before reloading or restarting NGINX:
Bash
sudo nginx -t


Important Considerations for "100% Lockdown":
The Human Factor: Even the most secure technical configurations can be undone by human error, weak passwords, or social engineering. Implement strong security policies, user training, and access control for administrators.
Application Security: NGINX can protect against many web-based threats, but it cannot fully compensate for vulnerabilities within your backend application code. Ensure your application itself is secure.
Third-Party Modules/Integrations: If you use any third-party NGINX modules or integrate with other services, thoroughly vet their security and keep them updated.
Complexity vs. Security: Overly complex configurations can introduce new vulnerabilities or make management difficult. Strive for a balance between strong security and maintainability.
Zero-Trust Model: Consider implementing a "zero-trust" security model where no user or device is inherently trusted, regardless of their location inside or outside the network.
By diligently implementing these measures, you can create a highly secure NGINX environment, significantly reducing its attack surface and protecting your web applications and data. Remember that security is an ongoing process, not a one-time setup.
